     "type": "intro",
     "title": "The Day Dropbox Stopped Buying Ads",
     "content": "In 2008, Sean Ellis joined a struggling file-sharing startup called Dropbox. They had a great product, but paid customer acquisition was punishing — Google AdWords cost $233 to acquire a customer for a $99/year product. Ellis killed the ad spend. Instead, he engineered a referral loop: give 500 MB free to anyone who invited a friend. In 15 months, signups jumped 60% and 35% of daily signups came from referrals. Dropbox grew from 100,000 to 4 million users without a marketing budget. Ellis later coined a new title for what he was doing — **growth hacker** — and an entire discipline was born. This lesson is your initiation into a way of thinking about growth that doesn't look like marketing at all.",
