     "type": "concept",
     "title": "What Is a Growth Hacker?",
     "content": "Sean Ellis defined a growth hacker as **a person whose true north is growth**. Everything they do is scrutinized by its potential impact on scalable growth.\n\nThe term is misleading — it isn't about clever tricks or hacks. It's a **methodology** that fuses:\n\n• **Marketing instincts** — understanding customers, channels, messaging\n• **Engineering capability** — building features, instrumentation, automation\n• **Data analysis** — interpreting cohorts, funnels, A/B tests\n• **Experimentation rigor** — testing hypotheses at speed\n\nA growth hacker doesn't manage a brand or run quarterly campaigns. They run **dozens of experiments per week**, each designed to move a specific metric. The output isn't a polished asset — it's learning, captured and compounded.",
