     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Growth Hacking vs Traditional Marketing",
     "content": "Traditional marketing and growth hacking pursue different goals on different time horizons.\n\n**Traditional marketing** asks: *How do we build brand awareness this quarter?* It produces campaigns, runs ads, measures impressions, and reports to a CMO. Cycle time is weeks to months.\n\n**Growth hacking** asks: *Which lever, tested this week, will move our north-star metric next week?* It produces experiments, ships features, measures conversions, and reports to whoever owns growth. Cycle time is days.\n\nThe key shift: marketing was historically **detached from the product**. Growth hackers see the product itself as the most powerful marketing channel. A signup flow, an onboarding tooltip, an empty-state CTA — these are growth levers, not just UX details.",
