     "type": "example",
     "title": "Hotmail's PS Line That Built a Billion-Dollar Exit",
     "content": "In 1996, Hotmail had 20,000 users and a marketing budget under $50,000. Investor Tim Draper suggested adding one line to the bottom of every outgoing email: **\"PS: I love you. Get your free email at Hotmail.\"**\n\nResults:\n• **Month 6**: 1 million users\n• **Month 18**: 12 million users (a tenth of all internet users at the time)\n• **December 1997**: Microsoft acquired Hotmail for $400 million\n\nThis is growth hacking before the term existed. The team didn't buy ads. They turned the product itself into the distribution channel. Every email sent was a recruiting pitch. The cost per acquired user was effectively zero, and the loop ran at the speed of the internet.\n\nNotice what this *isn't*: it isn't clever copy, it isn't a viral video, it isn't a campaign. It's an engineering decision that lives inside the product and compounds.",
