     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Marketing Is Now Engineering",
     "content": "The deepest shift Ellis describes is that **marketing has become an engineering discipline**. Where once a marketer's deliverable was a TV spot or a brochure, today the highest-leverage marketing work happens in code.\n\nConsider the modern stack:\n• **A/B testing platforms** (Optimizely, VWO) ship experimental code branches\n• **Behavioral analytics** (Mixpanel, Amplitude) instrument every user action\n• **Lifecycle email tools** (Customer.io, Iterable) trigger messages on engineering-defined events\n• **Referral systems** (Branch, Friendbuy) bake invitations into the product\n• **Personalization engines** customize landing pages per visitor in real time\n\nNone of these can be operated by a marketer who cannot speak engineering. The growth hacker is the **translation layer** between business goals and the codebase. This is why Ellis insists on **cross-functional teams** — a topic we'll go deep on in Lesson 3.",
