     "explanation": "This is the canonical example Ellis cites to make the retention case. With 1,000 new users per month, Product A ends at ~8,300 and Product B at ~13,200 — about 60% more. The intuition fails most teams because they reason linearly: \"5 points is small.\" But retention compounds — every month, B keeps a larger share of its accumulated base — so the gap widens nonlinearly. The exact magnitude depends on time horizon and acquisition, but 60% over 24 months for a 5-point delta is roughly correct.",
