     "explanation": "Ellis is explicit: K > 1 (true viral growth) is extraordinarily rare and usually short-lived. Most successful products run sub-viral loops in the 0.3-0.7 range. A K of 0.4 means every 10 users bring 4 more — those 4 cost the company effectively $0 in marketing. That is a massive win that compounds over years. Teams that abandon programs because K is below 1 throw away large CAC savings chasing a fairy-tale benchmark.",
