     "explanation": "The Tesla case illustrates Ellis's warning that incentives must align with **real motivation**. When the reward grew to a free Roadster ($200K), the program attracted affiliate marketers, not enthusiastic owners. Referred buyers would have bought anyway, so incrementality collapsed. K-factor wasn't the issue — the issue was that high K was generated by inauthentic shares. Tesla never moved to traditional advertising; they killed the program in 2019 because the math no longer worked. The lesson: keep incentives modest, in-product, and aligned with both parties' real motivations.",
