     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Common Failure Modes",
     "content": "Ellis closes *Hacking Growth* with a list of failure modes growth teams fall into. Recognizing them is the first step to avoiding them:\n\n• **The HiPPO trap** — Highest Paid Person's Opinion overrides data. The CEO insists on a redesign that the data says will fail. Cure: pre-committed experiment rules.\n\n• **Vanity metric obsession** — celebrating signups, page views, app downloads instead of activated users. Cure: locked NSM.\n\n• **Experimentation theatre** — running tests for the sake of running tests, with no underlying hypothesis. Cure: experiment spec discipline.\n\n• **One-channel addiction** — 80%+ from one source. Cure: diversification (Lesson 5).\n\n• **Activation neglect** — pouring acquisition fuel into a leaky funnel. Cure: PMF and aha discipline (Lessons 2 and 4).\n\n• **Premature scaling** — hiring a 20-person growth team before reaching PMF. Cure: stage-gate growth investment.\n\n• **Burnout** — running so many tests the team breaks. Cure: 3-7 tests per sprint, not 30. Sustainable cadence beats heroic bursts.\n\n• **Learning amnesia** — losing the institutional memory of past experiments. Cure: documented post-mortems, searchable wiki.\n\nEvery failure mode has a cure. The cures are organizational, not technical.",
