     "type": "intro",
     "title": "Principle Extraction, Not Tactic Copying",
     "content": "Three stories dominate growth hacking folklore: Hotmail's free email signature that turned every sent message into an advertisement, Dropbox's referral program that gave both parties extra storage, and Airbnb's early integration with Craigslist that borrowed another platform's audience. These stories get retold so often that they risk becoming cargo cult wisdom, where a founder copies the surface tactic, such as adding a referral button, without understanding why it worked in its original context. Literally copying 'add a signature to outgoing emails' onto a product where users rarely send anything on another person's behalf accomplishes nothing, because the mechanism that made Hotmail's hack work is not the specific words in the signature, it is the fact that using the product itself created a distribution event reaching new, well-targeted people at zero marginal cost. The discipline this lesson teaches is mechanism extraction: for each famous hack, separate the specific tactic, which is often time-bound, platform-specific, and eventually stops working, from the underlying structural principle, which is durable and transferable to a different product entirely. Understanding the principle behind Hotmail's signature helps a modern team ask, 'where in our product does normal usage already put our product in front of new, relevant people,' rather than literally asking whether to add a line of text to an email footer. The same discipline applies to Dropbox's referral mechanics and Airbnb's platform arbitrage, both examined in depth in this lesson alongside their lasting, generalizable lessons.",
