     "type": "example",
     "title": "Dropbox's Double-Sided Referral and Airbnb's Platform Arbitrage",
     "content": "Dropbox faced a different problem than Hotmail: file storage is not inherently visible to other people the way an email is. So rather than relying on incidental exposure, Dropbox built a deliberate, two-sided referral program: refer a friend, and both the referrer and the new signup receive extra storage space, up to a meaningful cap. Referrals reportedly drove a sustained 60 percent increase in signups during the program's active years. The mechanism worth extracting is incentive alignment paired with product-native rewards. Because both sides benefit, a user does not feel like they are imposing on a friend or acting as an unpaid salesperson, which removes the social friction and mild guilt that plagues one-sided referral schemes. Because the reward is extra storage rather than cash, Dropbox's cost per referral was tied to its own low marginal cost of storage rather than a real cash outlay, and the reward appealed specifically to the users most likely to want the product, existing power users bumping against their storage limit. Airbnb's Craigslist integration operated on an entirely different mechanism: platform arbitrage. In its early days, Airbnb built a tool letting hosts cross-post their listing to Craigslist with one click, meeting enormous existing demand for short-term housing exactly where that demand already congregated, rather than spending years building an audience from nothing. The generalizable principle is to identify adjacent platforms where your target users' intent is already concentrated, and meet them there rather than competing for cold attention. It is worth noting this specific tactic exploited an unofficial technical integration, drew scrutiny, and became unavailable once Craigslist changed its systems, illustrating that borrowed-platform tactics are often fragile and worth building on a sustainable, ideally sanctioned foundation.",
